# Base pins for the tool patches in this directory.
#
# Each patch was taken against the upstream commit below. `install-patches.sh` sources this file,
# verifies the located source checkout is at the recorded commit and git tree, verifies the sha256
# of each patch file itself, verifies the base sha256 of every file the patch modifies, and runs
# `git apply --check` against the base tree archived from the pinned commit. A wrong commit, wrong
# tree, wrong patch file, wrong base file or a hunk that does not apply exits nonzero.
#
# Refresh a pin after a deliberate rebase:
#   git -C <src> rev-parse HEAD HEAD^{tree}
#   git -C <src> show HEAD:<path> | sha256sum
#   sha256sum patches/<file>.patch
#
# CHARON_COMMIT / CHARON_TREE identify AeneasVerif/charon.
# AENEAS_COMMIT / AENEAS_TREE identify AeneasVerif/aeneas (its `charon/` is a separate checkout).
#
# *_PATCH_SHA256 pin the patch files themselves, so an edit to a hunk that is not a deliberate
# rebase is caught rather than silently changing the translator.
# AENEAS_TRANSLATOR_SHA256 / CHARON_SHA256 / CHARON_DRIVER_SHA256 pin the installed
# `$AENEAS_HOME/{aeneas,charon,charon-driver}`. Each is the digest of the pinned build after
# `strip -R .note.gnu.build-id`: `strip` removes the symbol table and the debug sections, in which
# OCaml records the build directory, and removing the note drops the content-derived build-id, so
# the pinned digest is a function of the source and toolchain and not of the build root.
# AENEAS_TRANSLATOR_SHA256 is the identity that translates this repository's `src/`; `regen.sh`
# asserts it (fail-closed) and `bootstrap.sh` asserts all three before installing.
